// Class 11 Biology practice bank, part 3: Human Physiology (Body Fluids, Excretion, Locomotion, Neural Control, Chemical Coordination).
StudyData.addPractice(11, "biology", {
  "body-fluids-and-circulation": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The plasma protein that is converted into insoluble threads during blood clotting is:", options: ["albumin", "globulin", "fibrinogen", "prothrombin"], answer: 2, explain: "Thrombin converts soluble fibrinogen into insoluble fibrin threads. Prothrombin is the inactive precursor of thrombin, not the protein that forms threads.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The normal blood pressure of a healthy adult is about:", options: ["80/120 mm Hg", "120/80 mm Hg", "140/90 mm Hg", "160/100 mm Hg"], answer: 1, explain: "Normal BP is 120 (systolic) over 80 (diastolic) mm Hg; 140/90 or above is high.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The valve between the left atrium and the left ventricle is the:", options: ["bicuspid (mitral) valve", "tricuspid valve", "pulmonary semilunar valve", "aortic semilunar valve"], answer: 0, explain: "The left AV opening is guarded by the bicuspid valve; the right one by the tricuspid valve.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Platelets (thrombocytes) are formed as fragments of:", options: ["lymphocytes", "erythroblasts", "monocytes", "megakaryocytes"], answer: 3, explain: "Platelets are cell fragments produced from megakaryocytes of the bone marrow.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Fats absorbed in the intestinal villi are carried mainly through the:", options: ["hepatic portal vein", "lacteals (lymph)", "coronary vein", "pulmonary vein"], answer: 1, explain: "Lymph in the lacteals of the villi carries absorbed fats.", level: 1 },

      { q: "At a blood-donation camp, a patient of blood group B urgently needs RBCs. Which donors can safely donate to this patient?", options: ["A and AB", "B and AB", "B and O", "A and O"], answer: 2, explain: "A group B patient has anti-A antibodies, so the donor RBCs must not carry antigen A. Groups B and O are safe.", level: 2 },
      { q: "A resting man has a heart rate of 75 beats per minute and a stroke volume of 70 mL. His cardiac output is about:", options: ["52.5 L/min", "0.525 L/min", "1.07 L/min", "5.25 L/min"], answer: 3, explain: "Cardiac output = 75 × 70 mL = 5250 mL/min = 5.25 L/min.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The sinoatrial node is called the pacemaker of the heart.\nReason (R): The SAN generates the maximum number of action potentials per minute (70–75), so it sets the rate for the whole heart.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The node with the fastest rhythm drives the others, which is why SAN is the pacemaker.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Serum can still form a clot readily when exposed to air.\nReason (R): Serum is plasma from which the clotting factors have been removed.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Serum lacks the clotting factors, so it cannot clot; A is false. R is a correct definition of serum.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "During a school race, Aman's heart rate rises from 72 to nearly 150 beats per minute and his cardiac output increases. Which explanation is correct?", options: ["Parasympathetic nerves stimulate the SAN to beat faster", "The heart stops being myogenic and is controlled by the skeletal muscles", "Neural signals through the medulla and sympathetic nerves, together with adrenal medullary hormones, raise heart rate and cardiac output", "A sudden rise in RBC count raises the stroke volume within seconds"], answer: 2, explain: "Heart activity is myogenic but is modified by the medulla through the autonomic system. Sympathetic nerves and adrenal medullary hormones increase rate and output; parasympathetic nerves decrease them.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Match the white blood cells in Column I with their functions in Column II.\nColumn I: (a) Neutrophils (b) Eosinophils (c) Basophils (d) Lymphocytes\nColumn II: (i) Resist infections and take part in allergic reactions (ii) Release histamine and other substances in inflammatory reactions (iii) Phagocytic, most abundant WBCs (iv) B and T cells responsible for immune responses", options: ["a-i, b-iii, c-ii, d-iv", "a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv", "a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv", "a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii"], answer: 2, explain: "Neutrophils are phagocytic and most abundant; eosinophils resist infection and are involved in allergy; basophils release histamine; lymphocytes (B and T) give immunity.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Select the INCORRECT statement about the nodal tissue of the human heart.", options: ["The bundle of His passes through the atrio-ventricular septum and divides into right and left bundles.", "The AV node lies in the upper right corner of the right atrium.", "Purkinje fibres spread the impulse through the walls of the ventricles.", "After the SAN fires, the two atria contract at nearly the same time."], answer: 1, explain: "The SAN is in the upper right corner of the right atrium; the AV node lies in the lower left corner of the right atrium near the atrio-ventricular septum.", level: 3 },
      { q: "An athlete has a resting cardiac output of 5.6 L/min and a stroke volume of 80 mL. Her heart rate and the duration of one cardiac cycle (to two decimal places) are:", options: ["80/min; 0.75 s", "56/min; 1.07 s", "70/min; 0.70 s", "70/min; 0.86 s"], answer: 3, explain: "Heart rate = 5600 mL ÷ 80 mL = 70 beats/min. One cycle = 60 ÷ 70 = 0.86 s.", level: 3 },
      { q: "An Rh-negative woman delivers her first, Rh-positive baby but is not given anti-Rh antibodies. What is the most likely result in a later pregnancy with an Rh-positive foetus?", options: ["Anti-Rh antibodies already formed in her blood cross the placenta and destroy the foetal RBCs", "The foetus makes anti-Rh antibodies that destroy the mother's RBCs", "Nothing happens because the placenta stops all antibodies", "The mother passes Rh antigens to the foetus through the placenta"], answer: 0, explain: "Foetal Rh-positive RBCs entering her blood at delivery sensitise the mother, who makes anti-Rh antibodies. In the next pregnancy these cross the placenta and cause erythroblastosis foetalis.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "What is serum?", a: "Serum is plasma from which the clotting factors (such as fibrinogen) have been removed.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the blood group called the universal recipient and give the reason.", a: "Group AB. Its RBCs carry both A and B antigens and the plasma has neither anti-A nor anti-B antibodies, so it can receive blood from all groups.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Define stroke volume and cardiac output. How are they related?", a: "Stroke volume is the volume of blood pumped by each ventricle in one beat (about 70 mL). Cardiac output is the volume pumped by each ventricle per minute: cardiac output = stroke volume × heart rate, about 5 L/min.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name the two heart sounds and state what produces each.", a: "The first sound (lub) is due to the closure of the tricuspid and bicuspid valves at the start of ventricular systole. The second sound (dup) is due to the closure of the semilunar valves at the end of ventricular systole.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Explain what the P wave, the QRS complex and the T wave of an ECG represent.", a: "(1) P wave: depolarisation of the atria, which leads to atrial contraction. (2) QRS complex: depolarisation of the ventricles, marking the start of ventricular systole. (3) T wave: return of the ventricles from the excited to the normal state (repolarisation); the end of the T wave marks the end of ventricular systole.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Distinguish between pulmonary and systemic circulation. Which special pathway carries blood from the intestine to the liver?", a: "(1) Pulmonary circulation: right ventricle → pulmonary artery → lungs (oxygenation) → pulmonary veins → left atrium. (2) Systemic circulation: left ventricle → aorta → body tissues → venae cavae → right atrium. (3) The hepatic portal system carries blood from the intestine to the liver through the hepatic portal vein before it returns to the heart.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name the five types of white blood cells and state one function each of any three of them.", a: "The five types are neutrophils, eosinophils, basophils, lymphocytes and monocytes. Neutrophils and monocytes are phagocytic; eosinophils resist infections and are associated with allergic reactions; basophils release histamine in inflammatory reactions; lymphocytes (B and T cells) are responsible for immunity.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the cardiac cycle: name its phases, give the approximate duration of each and state what happens in the heart.", a: "(1) The cardiac cycle is the sequence of events from the start of one heartbeat to the start of the next; at 72 beats/min it lasts about 0.8 s. (2) Atrial systole (about 0.1 s): both atria contract and push the remaining blood into the ventricles through the open AV valves. (3) Ventricular systole (about 0.3 s): the AV valves close (lub), pressure rises, the semilunar valves open and blood flows into the pulmonary artery and aorta; about 70 mL (stroke volume) leaves each ventricle. (4) Joint diastole (about 0.4 s): the semilunar valves close (dup), the heart muscle relaxes and blood returns to the atria and flows into the ventricles. (5) Cardiac output = 70 mL × 72 = about 5 L/min.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Describe the ABO system of blood groups. Tabulate the antigens and antibodies of each group, state which groups can donate to which, and explain why group O is the universal donor.", a: "(1) The ABO system depends on antigens A and B on the surface of RBCs (discovered by Landsteiner). (2) Group A: antigen A, anti-B antibody in plasma. Group B: antigen B, anti-A. Group AB: antigens A and B, no antibodies. Group O: no antigens, both anti-A and anti-B. (3) Compatibility: A receives from A and O; B receives from B and O; AB receives from A, B, AB and O; O receives only from O. (4) Group O RBCs have no antigens, so the recipient's antibodies cannot agglutinate them, making O the universal donor. (5) Group AB has no antibodies and can receive from all, so it is the universal recipient. Mismatched transfusion causes agglutination of the donor RBCs.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Mr Sharma, 55, who eats a lot of fried food, felt a sudden pain in his chest while climbing stairs. The pain eased with rest. His BP was 150/95 mm Hg. The doctor said that deposits inside his coronary arteries were narrowing them.\n(i) Name the condition of the arteries and the term for the chest pain.\n(ii) Is his blood pressure normal? Give the normal and the high-BP values.\n(iii) Name the substances that are deposited and suggest one preventive measure.", a: "(i) The condition is coronary artery disease (atherosclerosis); the chest pain is angina pectoris, which occurs when the heart muscle gets an inadequate supply of blood (oxygen).\n(ii) No. Normal BP is 120/80 mm Hg; a reading of 140/90 mm Hg or above is high blood pressure (hypertension), so 150/95 is high.\n(iii) Calcium, fat, cholesterol and fibrous tissue are deposited, narrowing the lumen. Preventive measure: a low-fat diet, regular exercise and avoiding smoking.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "excretory-products-and-their-elimination": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The excretory structures of an earthworm are the:", options: ["flame cells", "nephridia", "Malpighian tubules", "antennal glands"], answer: 1, explain: "Earthworms have nephridia; flame cells are in flatworms, Malpighian tubules in insects and green glands in prawns.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The structural and functional unit of the kidney is the:", options: ["glomerulus", "Bowman's capsule", "loop of Henle", "nephron"], answer: 3, explain: "The nephron (glomerulus, Bowman's capsule and the tubule) is the functional unit.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The cells of Bowman's capsule that leave filtration slits are called:", options: ["JG cells", "podocytes", "Leydig cells", "hepatocytes"], answer: 1, explain: "Podocytes are specialised epithelial cells arranged to leave slit pores for ultrafiltration.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The hormone that increases water reabsorption in the distal parts of the nephron and so prevents diuresis is:", options: ["aldosterone", "renin", "ADH (vasopressin)", "ANF"], answer: 2, explain: "ADH from the neurohypophysis makes the tubule more permeable to water. Aldosterone mainly retains Na⁺ (and water with it).", level: 1 },
      { q: "Animals that excrete ammonia as the main nitrogenous waste are called:", options: ["ureotelic", "ammonotelic", "uricotelic", "aminotelic"], answer: 1, explain: "Bony fishes, aquatic amphibians and aquatic insects are ammonotelic.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A kidney patient is put on haemodialysis. Her blood, to which heparin has been added, passes through a coiled cellophane tube surrounded by dialysing fluid. Which statement is correct?", options: ["The dialysing fluid is rich in urea so that urea enters the blood", "Heparin is added to the dialysing fluid to dissolve urea", "The dialysing fluid has the plasma composition but no nitrogenous wastes, so urea moves from the blood into the fluid", "Cellophane allows RBCs and proteins to move into the fluid"], answer: 2, explain: "The dialysing fluid matches plasma except for nitrogenous wastes, so wastes diffuse out of the blood. Heparin is an anticoagulant added to the blood.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which part of the nephron is impermeable to water but transports electrolytes actively or passively?", options: ["descending limb of the loop of Henle", "ascending limb of the loop of Henle", "proximal convoluted tubule", "Bowman's capsule"], answer: 1, explain: "The ascending limb is impermeable to water; the descending limb is permeable to water and almost impermeable to electrolytes.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): The collecting duct can produce urine more concentrated than blood plasma.\nReason (R): The collecting duct passes through the medulla, where the interstitial osmolarity rises up to about 1200 mOsm/L, drawing water out of the duct.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "The medullary osmotic gradient built by the counter-current mechanism pulls water out of the collecting duct, concentrating the urine.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Birds need very little water to eliminate their nitrogenous waste.\nReason (R): Birds excrete ammonia, which has very low toxicity.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "Birds are uricotelic; uric acid is a nearly dry, low-toxicity pellet that needs little water. Ammonia is very toxic and needs plenty of water, so R is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "After an hour of running on a hot day, a student has sweated heavily and drunk little water. Her body fluid volume falls. Which sequence correctly shows the body's response?", options: ["ANF is released, vessels dilate and a large volume of dilute urine is formed", "Aldosterone secretion falls, so Na⁺ is lost in urine", "Glomerular filtration rises to 180 L per hour", "Osmoreceptors are activated, ADH is released from the neurohypophysis, more water is reabsorbed and a small volume of concentrated urine forms"], answer: 3, explain: "A fall in body fluid volume stimulates osmoreceptors, which cause ADH release; ADH increases water reabsorption and prevents diuresis.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Match the excretory structure in Column I with the animal in Column II.\nColumn I: (a) Protonephridia (b) Nephridia (c) Malpighian tubules (d) Antennal glands\nColumn II: (i) Earthworm (ii) Cockroach (iii) Prawn (iv) Flatworms (Platyhelminthes)", options: ["a-i, b-iv, c-ii, d-iii", "a-iv, b-i, c-ii, d-iii", "a-iv, b-i, c-iii, d-ii", "a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii"], answer: 1, explain: "Flame cells (protonephridia) are in flatworms, nephridia in earthworms, Malpighian tubules in insects such as cockroach and antennal (green) glands in prawns.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Select the INCORRECT statement.", options: ["The glomerular filtration rate of a healthy person is about 125 mL/min, i.e. about 180 L per day.", "Nearly all glucose and amino acids in the filtrate are reabsorbed in the PCT.", "The descending limb of the loop of Henle is impermeable to water.", "The vasa recta runs parallel to the loop of Henle and is a minute vessel arising from the efferent arteriole."], answer: 2, explain: "The descending limb is permeable to water (and almost impermeable to electrolytes); it is the ascending limb that is impermeable to water.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A person's GFR is 125 mL/min and she passes 1.5 L of urine per day. About what percentage of the filtrate is reabsorbed?", options: ["about 90%", "about 95%", "about 99%", "about 50%"], answer: 2, explain: "Filtrate per day = 125 × 1440 mL = 180 L. Urine = 1.5 L, so 1.5 ÷ 180 ≈ 0.8% is excreted and about 99% is reabsorbed.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A fall in glomerular blood flow sets off a chain of events. Which is the correct order?", options: ["ANF is released from the atria, vasodilation occurs, then the adrenal cortex secretes aldosterone", "Aldosterone is secreted, which makes the JG cells release renin, which forms angiotensin II", "ADH is released from the adrenal cortex, then renin forms angiotensinogen", "JG cells release renin, angiotensinogen is converted to angiotensin I and then II, aldosterone is released from the adrenal cortex, Na⁺ and water are reabsorbed"], answer: 3, explain: "This is the renin–angiotensin mechanism: renin → angiotensin I → angiotensin II → aldosterone → Na⁺ and water reabsorption, which restores blood pressure and GFR.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the enzyme released by the JG cells when glomerular blood flow falls.", a: "Renin.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "What is the term for the presence of ketone bodies in urine?", a: "Ketonuria (seen along with glycosuria in diabetes mellitus).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between ureotelic and uricotelic animals with two examples each.", a: "Ureotelic animals excrete urea, which is less toxic than ammonia and needs moderate water, e.g. mammals and marine fishes. Uricotelic animals excrete uric acid as pellets or paste with very little water loss, e.g. birds and reptiles (also insects and land snails).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "State two functions of the proximal convoluted tubule (PCT).", a: "(1) Nearly all essential nutrients and 70–80% of the electrolytes and water of the filtrate are reabsorbed. (2) It selectively secretes H⁺, K⁺ and ammonia into the filtrate, helping to maintain the pH and ionic balance of body fluids.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe the layers through which ultrafiltration takes place in the renal corpuscle.", a: "(1) Blood is filtered through three layers: the endothelium of the glomerular capillaries, the basement membrane, and the epithelium of Bowman's capsule made of podocytes. (2) The podocytes are arranged so as to leave minute spaces called filtration slits. (3) All constituents of plasma except the proteins pass into the lumen of Bowman's capsule, hence the process is called ultrafiltration.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how micturition is controlled.", a: "(1) Urine formed by the nephrons is stored in the urinary bladder until a voluntary signal is given by the CNS. (2) As the bladder fills, stretch receptors in its wall send signals to the CNS. (3) The CNS sends motor messages that cause contraction of the smooth muscles of the bladder and relaxation of the urethral sphincter, so urine is passed out.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name three organs other than the kidneys that help in excretion and the wastes each removes.", a: "(1) Lungs remove large amounts of CO₂ and also some water. (2) Liver secretes bile containing bilirubin, biliverdin, cholesterol and degraded steroid hormones, vitamins and drugs. (3) Skin: sweat glands remove NaCl, small amounts of urea and lactic acid; sebaceous glands remove sterols, hydrocarbons and waxes.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the three steps of urine formation in the nephron and the part played by each region of the nephron.", a: "(1) Glomerular filtration: about 1100–1200 mL of blood is filtered per minute and the GFR is about 125 mL/min (about 180 L/day) across the three-layered filtration membrane. (2) Reabsorption in the PCT: nearly all essential nutrients and 70–80% of electrolytes and water. (3) Loop of Henle: minimal reabsorption, but it maintains a high osmolarity in the medullary interstitium. (4) DCT: conditional reabsorption of Na⁺ and water, reabsorption of HCO₃⁻ and selective secretion of H⁺, K⁺ and NH₃. (5) Collecting duct: reabsorbs large amounts of water to form concentrated urine and allows a little urea into the medullary interstitium. Tubular secretion of H⁺, K⁺ and ammonia adjusts the final composition.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain how the counter-current mechanism produces concentrated urine. What is the role of the vasa recta?", a: "(1) The two limbs of the loop of Henle run in opposite directions, and so do the two limbs of the vasa recta, lying close together; hence 'counter-current'. (2) The descending limb of the loop is permeable to water, so the filtrate becomes more concentrated as it goes down; the ascending limb is impermeable to water but transports electrolytes out. (3) This builds an osmolarity gradient from about 300 mOsm/L in the cortex to about 1200 mOsm/L in the inner medulla, mainly due to NaCl and urea. (4) NaCl moves from the ascending limb to the descending limb of the vasa recta and is returned to the interstitium by its ascending part; small amounts of urea enter the ascending limb and are returned by the collecting duct. (5) The vasa recta thus helps to maintain the gradient; water leaves the collecting duct along it, so the urine becomes about four times more concentrated than the initial filtrate.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A 45-year-old man with diabetes passes urine very often. A urine test shows glucose and ketone bodies. Another patient, an elderly woman, has severe pain in the lower back with blood in the urine; a scan shows a hard mass of crystallised salts in her kidney. A third patient with kidney failure has a high level of urea in blood and is advised dialysis.\n(i) Name the terms for glucose and ketone bodies in urine.\n(ii) Name the condition of the elderly woman.\n(iii) Name the condition in the third patient and two ways of treating it.", a: "(i) Glucose in urine is glycosuria; ketone bodies in urine is ketonuria.\n(ii) Renal calculi: a stone or insoluble mass of crystallised salts (such as oxalates) formed within the kidney.\n(iii) Uraemia: accumulation of urea in the blood, which may lead to kidney failure. It is treated by haemodialysis (an artificial kidney removes urea from blood) or by kidney transplantation, ideally from a close relative to reduce rejection.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "locomotion-and-movement": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The movement shown by macrophages and leucocytes using pseudopodia is called:", options: ["ciliary movement", "muscular movement", "amoeboid movement", "flagellar movement"], answer: 2, explain: "Pseudopodia formed by the streaming of protoplasm (with the help of microfilaments) give amoeboid movement.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Which type of muscle is striated but involuntary?", options: ["skeletal muscle", "cardiac muscle", "smooth muscle", "visceral muscle"], answer: 1, explain: "Cardiac muscle is striated and involuntary; skeletal is striated and voluntary; visceral (smooth) is non-striated and involuntary.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The plasma membrane of a muscle fibre is called the:", options: ["sarcoplasm", "sarcomere", "sarcoplasmic reticulum", "sarcolemma"], answer: 3, explain: "The sarcolemma is the muscle fibre's membrane; the sarcoplasm is its cytoplasm and the sarcoplasmic reticulum stores Ca²⁺.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The total number of bones in the adult human skeleton is:", options: ["206", "186", "300", "80"], answer: 0, explain: "There are 206 bones: 80 in the axial and 126 in the appendicular skeleton.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The knee joint is an example of a:", options: ["gliding joint", "pivot joint", "hinge joint", "saddle joint"], answer: 2, explain: "The knee is a hinge joint (movement in one plane); the shoulder is ball and socket.", level: 1 },

      { q: "A marathon runner can keep running for hours, while an untrained person tires quickly. Which statement correctly describes the muscle fibres that suit the runner's long, steady effort?", options: ["They are mostly white fibres that store glycogen", "They are white fibres that use only aerobic respiration", "They are red fibres that have very little myoglobin", "They are red fibres with abundant myoglobin and many mitochondria, working aerobically"], answer: 3, explain: "Red (aerobic) fibres have plenty of myoglobin and mitochondria for sustained work. White fibres have less myoglobin and depend mainly on anaerobic processes.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which event comes FIRST in the contraction of a skeletal muscle fibre?", options: ["Ca²⁺ binds to troponin", "Acetylcholine is released at the neuromuscular junction", "Cross-bridges form between myosin heads and actin", "Ca²⁺ is released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum"], answer: 1, explain: "The sequence is: signal from motor neuron → acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction → action potential in the sarcolemma → Ca²⁺ release → binding to troponin → cross-bridges.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): During muscle contraction the length of the A band does not change.\nReason (R): The A band is made of the thick myosin filaments, which keep their length while the thin filaments slide over them.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "In the sliding filament theory only the overlap changes; thick filaments stay the same length, so the A band is constant.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Low calcium in body fluids causes tetany, rapid spasms of muscles.\nReason (R): Ca²⁺ released from the sarcoplasmic reticulum binds to troponin during normal muscle contraction.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 1, explain: "Both statements are true, but R describes the normal role of Ca²⁺ in contraction inside the fibre; it does not explain why a fall of Ca²⁺ in body fluid produces spasms.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "Kamla, 68, a woman past menopause, fractured her hip after a minor fall. Her scan showed a marked loss of bone mass. The most likely disorder, in which a fall in oestrogen is a common cause, is:", options: ["gout", "arthritis", "osteoporosis", "tetany"], answer: 2, explain: "Osteoporosis is an age-related disorder with decreased bone mass and increased chances of fractures; decreased oestrogen is a common cause.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Match the disorder in Column I with its description in Column II.\nColumn I: (a) Myasthenia gravis (b) Tetany (c) Muscular dystrophy (d) Gout\nColumn II: (i) Rapid spasms due to low Ca²⁺ in body fluid (ii) Autoimmune disorder affecting the neuromuscular junction (iii) Progressive degeneration of skeletal muscle, mostly genetic (iv) Joint inflammation due to uric acid crystals", options: ["a-i, b-ii, c-iii, d-iv", "a-iii, b-i, c-ii, d-iv", "a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv", "a-ii, b-i, c-iii, d-iv"], answer: 3, explain: "Myasthenia gravis is autoimmune and hits the neuromuscular junction; tetany is due to low Ca²⁺; muscular dystrophy is genetic degeneration; gout is due to uric acid crystals.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Select the INCORRECT statement.", options: ["Each coxal bone of the pelvic girdle is formed by the fusion of ilium, ischium and pubis.", "The last two pairs of ribs, the floating ribs, are not connected ventrally.", "The glenoid cavity receives the head of the femur.", "The skull has 8 cranial bones and 14 facial bones."], answer: 2, explain: "The glenoid cavity of the scapula receives the head of the humerus; the head of the femur fits into the acetabulum of the pelvic girdle.", level: 3 },
      { q: "The appendicular skeleton has 126 bones. If each of the four limbs has 30 bones and the pectoral girdle has 4 bones (two clavicles and two scapulae), how many bones are in the pelvic girdle?", options: ["1", "2", "4", "6"], answer: 1, explain: "126 − (4 × 30) − 4 = 2; the pelvic girdle consists of the two coxal bones.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which set of changes occurs in a sarcomere when a skeletal muscle fibre contracts?", options: ["A band remains unchanged; the I band and H zone shorten; the Z lines move closer", "The A band shortens while the I band stays unchanged", "The H zone widens and the I band shortens", "Both thick and thin filaments become shorter"], answer: 0, explain: "In sliding of filaments the thin filaments are pulled inward over the thick ones. The filaments keep their length, so the A band is constant, the I band and H zone narrow and the Z lines come closer.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the protein that forms the thick filaments of a myofibril.", a: "Myosin (the thin filaments are made of actin).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the bone that bears the socket for the head of the femur and the bones that fuse to form it.", a: "The acetabulum, a cavity in the coxal bone, which is formed by the fusion of the ilium, ischium and pubis.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the three types of joints with one example of each.", a: "Fibrous joints (no movement), e.g. sutures between the skull bones. Cartilaginous joints (limited movement), e.g. joints between adjacent vertebrae. Synovial joints (free movement, with a fluid-filled cavity), e.g. the knee or shoulder.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "What is gout? Name the substance responsible and one other disorder of joints.", a: "Gout is the inflammation of joints due to the accumulation of uric acid crystals. Another joint disorder is arthritis (inflammation of joints).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name the three types of movement seen in the human body with one example each.", a: "(1) Amoeboid movement: by macrophages and leucocytes, using pseudopodia formed by microfilaments. (2) Ciliary movement: of the cilia lining the trachea and in the passage of the ovum through the female reproductive tract. (3) Muscular movement: of the limbs, jaws and tongue.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Describe the structure of a sarcomere, naming its bands and lines.", a: "(1) A sarcomere is the part of a myofibril between two successive Z lines; it is the functional unit of contraction. (2) The dark A band (thick myosin filaments, with overlapping actin) lies in the middle, with a lighter H zone at its centre bearing the M line. (3) The light I bands (thin actin filaments only) lie on either side and are bisected by the Z lines, which anchor the thin filaments.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Distinguish among skeletal, smooth and cardiac muscle in terms of striations, control and location.", a: "(1) Skeletal muscle: striated, voluntary, attached to bones. (2) Smooth (visceral) muscle: non-striated, involuntary, in the walls of hollow visceral organs such as the alimentary canal. (3) Cardiac muscle: striated, involuntary, in the heart, with intercalated discs joining the cells.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the sliding filament theory of muscle contraction, with the role of Ca²⁺ and ATP.", a: "(1) A signal from the CNS through a motor neuron releases acetylcholine at the neuromuscular junction, producing an action potential in the sarcolemma. (2) This releases Ca²⁺ from the sarcoplasmic reticulum into the sarcoplasm. (3) Ca²⁺ binds troponin, which exposes the active sites on actin for myosin. (4) The myosin head, energised by ATP hydrolysis, binds to actin to form a cross-bridge and pulls the thin filament towards the centre of the sarcomere (power stroke); ATP binding releases the head so that the cycle repeats. (5) Thin filaments slide over thick ones, so the I band and H zone shorten while the A band stays the same. Ca²⁺ is pumped back, the active sites are covered again and the muscle relaxes.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Give the composition of the human skeleton: state the number of bones in the axial and appendicular skeletons and the parts of each.", a: "(1) An adult has 206 bones: axial skeleton 80 and appendicular skeleton 126. (2) Axial: skull 29 (8 cranial, 14 facial, 1 hyoid, 6 ear ossicles), vertebral column 26 (7 cervical, 12 thoracic, 5 lumbar, 1 sacral, 1 coccygeal), sternum 1 and 24 ribs (12 pairs: 7 true, 3 false, 2 floating). (3) Appendicular: pectoral girdle (clavicle and scapula, with the glenoid cavity) and pelvic girdle (two coxal bones with the acetabulum). (4) Each forelimb has 30 bones: humerus, radius, ulna, 8 carpals, 5 metacarpals and 14 phalanges. (5) Each hindlimb has 30 bones: femur, tibia, fibula, patella, 7 tarsals, 5 metatarsals and 14 phalanges.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: A 12-year-old boy is gradually losing strength in his muscles and finds it hard to climb stairs; the doctor says his skeletal muscles are degenerating and the cause is genetic. A 20-year-old girl has sudden painful spasms of her hands and feet, and her report shows a low level of calcium in the body fluids. An elderly man has swollen, painful toe joints, and his blood has a high level of uric acid.\n(i) Name the boy's disorder.\n(ii) Name the girl's disorder and its cause.\n(iii) Name the elderly man's disorder and the substance deposited in the joints.", a: "(i) Muscular dystrophy: progressive degeneration of skeletal muscle, mostly due to a genetic disorder.\n(ii) Tetany: rapid spasms (wild contractions) of muscles caused by a low level of Ca²⁺ in the body fluid.\n(iii) Gout: inflammation of joints due to the accumulation of uric acid crystals.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "neural-control-and-coordination": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "The dendrites of a neuron transmit nerve impulses:", options: ["away from the cell body", "towards the cell body", "directly to the muscle fibre", "only across electrical synapses"], answer: 1, explain: "Dendrites carry impulses towards the cell body (cyton); the axon carries them away.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The three meninges covering the brain, from the outermost inwards, are:", options: ["pia mater, arachnoid, dura mater", "arachnoid, dura mater, pia mater", "dura mater, arachnoid, pia mater", "dura mater, pia mater, arachnoid"], answer: 2, explain: "Dura mater is the tough outer layer, the arachnoid is in the middle and the pia mater lies on the brain.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Balance and posture of the body are maintained mainly by the:", options: ["cerebrum", "medulla oblongata", "thalamus", "cerebellum"], answer: 3, explain: "The cerebellum, with its highly convoluted surface, controls balance and posture; the vestibular apparatus of the ear also helps.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The organ of Corti, the receptor for hearing, is located in the:", options: ["cochlea", "utricle", "tympanic membrane", "semicircular canal"], answer: 0, explain: "The organ of Corti rests on the basilar membrane inside the coiled cochlea.", level: 1 },
      { q: "In a chemical synapse the neurotransmitter is stored in:", options: ["Nissl's granules", "mitochondria", "synaptic vesicles", "the myelin sheath"], answer: 2, explain: "Neurotransmitters are packed in synaptic vesicles in the axon terminal and are released into the synaptic cleft.", level: 1 },

      { q: "Ravi touches a hot pan and withdraws his hand before he feels the pain. Which pathway shows how the response occurs?", options: ["receptor → motor neuron → brain → sensory neuron → muscle", "receptor → sensory (afferent) neuron → spinal cord → motor (efferent) neuron → effector muscle", "receptor → spinal cord → sensory neuron → muscle → brain", "effector → motor neuron → spinal cord → sensory neuron → receptor"], answer: 1, explain: "A reflex arc is receptor → afferent neuron → CNS (here the spinal cord) → efferent neuron → effector. The brain is informed afterwards.", level: 2 },
      { q: "During the depolarisation phase of an action potential in an axon, the main ion movement is:", options: ["K⁺ out of the axon", "Na⁺ out of the axon", "K⁺ into the axon", "Na⁺ into the axon"], answer: 3, explain: "On stimulation the membrane becomes highly permeable to Na⁺, which rushes in and reverses the polarity. Repolarisation follows as K⁺ moves out.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Rods are responsible for twilight (dim-light) vision.\nReason (R): Rods contain the pigment rhodopsin (visual purple), which responds to low light intensity.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "Rhodopsin, a derivative of vitamin A, makes rods sensitive to dim light; cones with photopsins serve day and colour vision.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): A chemical synapse conducts the impulse in only one direction.\nReason (R): Neurotransmitters are released from the post-synaptic membrane and act on receptors on the pre-synaptic membrane.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 2, explain: "Transmitter is released from the pre-synaptic membrane and acts on the post-synaptic membrane, so transmission is one-way. R states the reverse and is false.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "After a road accident a man can think and speak normally, but he cannot control his breathing and heartbeat properly, and a scan shows damage at the base of the brain. Which part is most likely damaged?", options: ["cerebellum", "corpus callosum", "hypothalamus", "medulla oblongata"], answer: 3, explain: "The medulla oblongata contains centres for the control of respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Match the brain part in Column I with its function or feature in Column II.\nColumn I: (a) Hypothalamus (b) Cerebellum (c) Corpora quadrigemina (d) Corpus callosum\nColumn II: (i) Connects the two cerebral hemispheres (ii) Regulates hunger, thirst and body temperature (iii) Controls balance and posture (iv) Reflex centres for vision and hearing", options: ["a-ii, b-i, c-iv, d-iii", "a-iii, b-ii, c-iv, d-i", "a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i", "a-iv, b-iii, c-ii, d-i"], answer: 2, explain: "The hypothalamus controls hunger, thirst and temperature; the cerebellum balance; the corpora quadrigemina of the midbrain visual and auditory reflexes; the corpus callosum joins the hemispheres.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Select the INCORRECT statement about the ear.", options: ["The stapes is attached to the oval window.", "The Eustachian tube connects the middle ear cavity with the pharynx.", "The hair cells of the organ of Corti are in contact with the tectorial membrane.", "The membranous labyrinth is filled with perilymph."], answer: 3, explain: "The membranous labyrinth contains endolymph; the perilymph surrounds it in the bony labyrinth.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which is the correct order in which a sound wave is converted into a nerve impulse?", options: ["Tympanic membrane → malleus → incus → stapes → oval window → cochlear fluid → basilar membrane → hair cells", "Tympanic membrane → stapes → incus → malleus → oval window → cochlear fluid → hair cells", "Oval window → tympanic membrane → ossicles → basilar membrane → hair cells", "Pinna → ossicles → tympanic membrane → cochlear fluid → hair cells"], answer: 0, explain: "The eardrum vibrates, the ossicles (malleus, incus, stapes) amplify the vibration to the oval window, the cochlear fluid sets the basilar membrane moving and the hair cells bend against the tectorial membrane, starting impulses in the auditory nerve.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A drug blocks the voltage-gated Na⁺ channels of an axon but does not affect the Na⁺/K⁺ pump. What happens when the axon is stimulated?", options: ["Repolarisation fails but depolarisation proceeds normally", "Depolarisation does not occur, so no action potential is generated or conducted", "The resting potential becomes positive inside", "The membrane becomes permanently depolarised"], answer: 1, explain: "Rapid Na⁺ entry causes depolarisation, which is the start of the action potential. With the channels blocked there is no depolarisation, so no impulse arises and none is conducted.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Name the part of the brain that contains the centres for control of respiration and cardiovascular reflexes.", a: "The medulla oblongata (of the hindbrain).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the ear ossicles in order from the tympanic membrane inwards.", a: "Malleus, incus and stapes.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Differentiate between myelinated and non-myelinated nerve fibres.", a: "Myelinated fibres are enveloped by Schwann cells that form a myelin sheath around the axon, with gaps called nodes of Ranvier between adjacent sheaths; they occur in spinal and cranial nerves. Non-myelinated fibres are enclosed by a Schwann cell that does not form a myelin sheath; they are common in the autonomic and somatic neural systems.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Differentiate between an electrical and a chemical synapse.", a: "At an electrical synapse the membranes of the pre- and post-synaptic neurons are very close, so the current flows directly through them and transmission is faster; such synapses are rare in our system. At a chemical synapse a fluid-filled synaptic cleft separates the membranes, and neurotransmitters released from the pre-synaptic neuron bind receptors on the post-synaptic membrane to generate a new potential.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Describe the reflex arc using the withdrawal of the hand from a hot object.", a: "(1) The heat is detected by the receptors (sense organs) in the skin. (2) The afferent (sensory) neuron carries the impulse to the spinal cord (CNS), where it passes to the efferent (motor) neuron. (3) The efferent neuron carries the impulse to the effector, the muscle of the arm, which contracts and withdraws the hand. The response is quick and involuntary.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name the three parts of the hindbrain and state one function of each.", a: "(1) Pons: has fibre tracts that interconnect different regions of the brain. (2) Cerebellum: has a very convoluted surface and controls balance and posture. (3) Medulla oblongata: connected to the spinal cord; has centres controlling respiration, cardiovascular reflexes and gastric secretions.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Distinguish between the blind spot and the fovea (macula lutea) of the eye.", a: "(1) The blind spot is the point where the optic nerve and blood vessels leave the eye; it has no photoreceptors (rods or cones), so no vision is possible there. (2) The macula lutea is a yellow pigmented spot near the posterior pole of the retina, with the fovea at its centre. (3) The fovea has only cones in the maximum density and is the region of the sharpest (most acute) vision.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain how a nerve impulse is generated and conducted along a neuron, starting from the resting potential.", a: "(1) At rest the axon membrane is polarised: the axoplasm has high K⁺ and negatively charged proteins and low Na⁺, the outside has high Na⁺ and low K⁺, and the outside is positive and the inside negative. (2) The Na⁺/K⁺ pump moves 3 Na⁺ out for 2 K⁺ in, maintaining this state. (3) A stimulus makes the membrane freely permeable to Na⁺; Na⁺ rushes in and the polarity is reversed (depolarisation), giving the action potential. (4) Soon the membrane becomes permeable to K⁺ again, K⁺ diffuses out and the resting state is restored (repolarisation). (5) The depolarised site acts as a stimulus for the adjacent region, so the impulse travels along the axon.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Describe how sound waves are converted into nerve impulses, naming the parts of the ear involved.", a: "(1) The pinna collects sound waves and directs them along the external auditory canal to the tympanic membrane, which vibrates. (2) In the middle ear the ossicles malleus, incus and stapes pass on and amplify the vibrations to the oval window (the Eustachian tube equalises air pressure). (3) The vibrations set up waves in the fluid of the cochlea of the inner ear. (4) These cause ripples in the basilar membrane, so the hair cells of the organ of Corti bend against the tectorial membrane. (5) This generates impulses in the associated afferent neurons, which are carried by the auditory nerve to the auditory cortex of the brain.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: At a junction between two neurons, an impulse reaches the axon terminal of the first neuron. Vesicles in the terminal fuse with the membrane and release acetylcholine into a narrow fluid-filled gap. The acetylcholine binds to receptors on the second neuron and a new impulse starts there.\n(i) Name this type of synapse and the gap.\n(ii) Why does the impulse travel only from the first neuron to the second?\n(iii) What is the role of the receptors on the second neuron?", a: "(i) A chemical synapse; the gap is the synaptic cleft.\n(ii) The neurotransmitter is stored in synaptic vesicles only in the axon terminal of the pre-synaptic neuron and acts on the receptors of the post-synaptic membrane, so transmission is one-way.\n(iii) The receptors bind the neurotransmitter, which opens ion channels in the post-synaptic membrane and generates a new potential (excitatory or inhibitory).", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  },
  "chemical-coordination-and-integration": {
    mcq: [
      { q: "Insulin is secreted by the:", options: ["α-cells of the islets of Langerhans", "δ-cells of the stomach", "Leydig cells of the testis", "β-cells of the islets of Langerhans"], answer: 3, explain: "β-cells of the islets of Langerhans secrete insulin; α-cells secrete glucagon.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Iodine is required for the synthesis of:", options: ["thyroxine and triiodothyronine", "insulin", "cortisol", "oxytocin"], answer: 0, explain: "T₄ and T₃ are iodinated hormones (iodothyronines); iodine deficiency causes simple goitre.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Oxytocin acts on:", options: ["the thyroid follicles", "the adrenal cortex", "the smooth muscles of the uterus and the mammary glands", "the liver cells"], answer: 2, explain: "Oxytocin causes uterine contractions in childbirth and milk ejection from the mammary glands.", level: 1 },
      { q: "Excess secretion of growth hormone in an adult causes:", options: ["gigantism", "acromegaly", "cretinism", "pituitary dwarfism"], answer: 1, explain: "In adults excess GH causes acromegaly (abnormal growth of face and hands); in childhood it causes gigantism.", level: 1 },
      { q: "The hormone secreted by the atrial wall of the heart is:", options: ["gastrin", "secretin", "erythropoietin", "atrial natriuretic factor"], answer: 3, explain: "ANF is secreted by the atria; it dilates blood vessels and lowers blood pressure.", level: 1 },

      { q: "In a hill village where the soil and water lack iodine, many people, including a girl named Ritu, have a swelling in the front of the neck. The most likely cause is:", options: ["excess growth hormone", "excess thyroxine from a tumour", "iodine deficiency leading to hypothyroidism and an enlarged thyroid (simple goitre)", "deficiency of parathormone"], answer: 2, explain: "Deficiency of iodine lowers thyroid hormone synthesis (hypothyroidism) and the gland enlarges, causing simple goitre.", level: 2 },
      { q: "Which statement about calcium regulation is correct?", options: ["Parathormone lowers blood Ca²⁺ by depositing it in bone", "Parathormone raises blood Ca²⁺ by acting on bones, kidneys and the intestine, while thyrocalcitonin lowers it", "Thyrocalcitonin raises blood Ca²⁺ by dissolving bone", "Both parathormone and thyrocalcitonin raise blood Ca²⁺"], answer: 1, explain: "PTH is the hypercalcemic hormone (bone dissolution, renal reabsorption, intestinal absorption); TCT is the opposing hormone that lowers blood Ca²⁺.", level: 2 },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Insulin is a hypoglycaemic hormone.\nReason (R): Insulin increases the uptake of glucose by cells and the conversion of glucose into glycogen in hepatocytes.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 0, explain: "By moving glucose into cells and promoting glycogenesis, insulin lowers blood glucose.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      {
        q: "Assertion (A): Steroid hormones generally act by binding to receptors on the cell surface and producing cyclic AMP.\nReason (R): Steroid hormones usually bind intracellular receptors and regulate gene expression.",
        options: ["Both A and R are true, and R is the correct explanation of A.", "Both A and R are true, but R is not the correct explanation of A.", "A is true, but R is false.", "A is false, but R is true."],
        answer: 3, explain: "Hormones with membrane-bound receptors (peptides, proteins) use second messengers like cAMP; steroid hormones act through intracellular receptors. So A is false and R is true.", level: 2, keepOrder: true
      },
      { q: "A man is always thirsty and passes a large volume of very dilute urine every day. His blood sugar is normal and his urine has no glucose. The most likely cause is:", options: ["diabetes insipidus due to deficiency of ADH", "diabetes mellitus due to deficiency of insulin", "acromegaly due to excess GH", "hyperthyroidism"], answer: 0, explain: "Lack of ADH impairs water reabsorption by the kidney, causing loss of water in urine and dehydration. In diabetes mellitus the urine would contain glucose.", level: 2 },

      { q: "Match the gastrointestinal hormone in Column I with its action in Column II.\nColumn I: (a) Gastrin (b) Secretin (c) Cholecystokinin (d) GIP\nColumn II: (i) Release of pancreatic enzymes and bile (ii) HCl and pepsinogen secretion by gastric glands (iii) Water and bicarbonate secretion by the exocrine pancreas (iv) Inhibits gastric secretion and motility", options: ["a-ii, b-i, c-iii, d-iv", "a-ii, b-iii, c-i, d-iv", "a-iii, b-ii, c-i, d-iv", "a-ii, b-iii, c-iv, d-i"], answer: 1, explain: "Gastrin acts on gastric glands; secretin on the exocrine pancreas for water and bicarbonate; CCK on pancreas and gall bladder; GIP inhibits gastric secretion and motility.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Select the INCORRECT statement.", options: ["Thymosins promote the differentiation of T-lymphocytes.", "Glucagon stimulates glycogenolysis and so raises blood glucose.", "The adrenal medulla secretes cortisol and aldosterone.", "Oestrogen is produced by the growing follicles and progesterone by the corpus luteum."], answer: 2, explain: "Cortisol and aldosterone are secreted by the adrenal cortex; the medulla secretes adrenaline and noradrenaline.", level: 3 },
      { q: "A tumour of the anterior pituitary secretes too much ACTH. Which change is expected?", options: ["Decreased secretion of thyroxine", "Increased secretion of aldosterone from the adrenal medulla", "Decreased secretion of cortisol", "Increased secretion of cortisol from the adrenal cortex, with raised gluconeogenesis"], answer: 3, explain: "ACTH stimulates the adrenal cortex to secrete steroids such as cortisol, which stimulates gluconeogenesis.", level: 3 },
      { q: "Which set contains only hormones that act through intracellular receptors and regulate gene expression?", options: ["Thyroxine, cortisol, testosterone", "Insulin, glucagon, thyroxine", "Adrenaline, cortisol, FSH", "Oxytocin, ADH, aldosterone"], answer: 0, explain: "Steroids (cortisol, testosterone) and iodothyronines (thyroxine) use intracellular receptors. Insulin, glucagon, adrenaline, FSH, oxytocin and ADH act through membrane-bound receptors.", level: 3 }
    ],
    written: [
      { q: "Which hormone of the thymus promotes differentiation of T-lymphocytes?", a: "Thymosin(s).", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Name the hormone secreted by the kidney that stimulates the formation of RBCs.", a: "Erythropoietin.", marks: 1 },
      { q: "Distinguish between gigantism and acromegaly.", a: "Both are caused by excess growth hormone. In gigantism the over-secretion occurs in childhood and the person grows abnormally tall; in acromegaly it occurs in adults and causes abnormal growth of the face and hands (extremities).", marks: 2 },
      { q: "Name two hormones of the adrenal medulla and give two effects they produce.", a: "Adrenaline (epinephrine) and noradrenaline (norepinephrine). They increase alertness, heart rate and strength of heart contraction, rate of respiration, sweating, pupillary dilation and piloerection, and stimulate the breakdown of glycogen, raising blood glucose.", marks: 2 },
      { q: "How does the hypothalamus control the secretion of the pituitary hormones? Give examples.", a: "(1) The hypothalamus makes releasing hormones, which stimulate the pituitary to secrete hormones, e.g. gonadotropin releasing hormone (GnRH) stimulates the release of LH and FSH. (2) It makes inhibiting hormones, e.g. somatostatin, which inhibits the release of growth hormone. (3) Oxytocin and vasopressin are made by the hypothalamic neurons and are transported along the axons to the neurohypophysis, from where they are released.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "State the sources and functions of the hormones testosterone, oestrogen and progesterone.", a: "(1) Testosterone (androgen) is secreted by the Leydig cells of the testis; it stimulates the development of male accessory sex organs and secondary sex characters and spermatogenesis. (2) Oestrogen is secreted by the growing ovarian follicles; it stimulates growth of the female secondary sex organs, follicle development and the female secondary sex characters. (3) Progesterone is secreted by the corpus luteum; it supports pregnancy and acts on mammary glands, causing formation of alveoli and milk secretion.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Explain the roles of parathormone and thyrocalcitonin in maintaining blood calcium.", a: "(1) Parathormone (PTH) from the parathyroid glands increases blood Ca²⁺ by stimulating the dissolution of bone, increasing reabsorption of Ca²⁺ by the renal tubules and increasing absorption from digested food. (2) Thyrocalcitonin (TCT) from the thyroid lowers blood Ca²⁺ level. (3) Working in opposite ways, PTH and TCT keep the calcium level within the normal range.", marks: 3 },
      { q: "Name the hormones of the adenohypophysis and state the function of each.", a: "(1) Growth hormone (GH): promotes body growth; excess causes gigantism or acromegaly, deficiency dwarfism. (2) Prolactin (PRL): growth of the mammary glands and milk formation. (3) TSH: stimulates the thyroid to make thyroid hormones. (4) ACTH: stimulates the adrenal cortex to make steroids. (5) LH and FSH: LH stimulates androgen synthesis from Leydig cells in males and causes ovulation and corpus luteum maintenance in females; FSH with androgens regulates spermatogenesis in males and stimulates follicular growth in females. MSH (pars intermedia) acts on melanocytes and regulates pigmentation.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Explain the mechanism of hormone action through membrane-bound and intracellular receptors, giving examples.", a: "(1) Hormones act on target tissues through specific receptors; the hormone-receptor complex starts the response. (2) Membrane-bound receptors are used by peptide, polypeptide and protein hormones and amino-acid derivatives (e.g. glucagon, adrenaline); these hormones do not enter the cell. (3) The hormone-receptor complex produces second messengers such as cyclic AMP, IP₃ and Ca²⁺, which regulate cellular metabolism. (4) Intracellular receptors are used by steroid hormones (e.g. cortisol, testosterone) and iodothyronines (T₃, T₄), which enter the cell. (5) The hormone-receptor complex interacts with the genome and regulates gene expression, resulting in physiological and developmental effects.", marks: 5 },
      { q: "Case study: Sanjay, 14, drinks large amounts of water, feels very hungry and is losing weight. A fasting blood test shows a very high glucose level, and his urine contains glucose and ketone bodies. The doctor says that his pancreas is not secreting enough of a particular hormone.\n(i) Name the disorder and the hormone that is deficient.\n(ii) Which cells secrete this hormone, and how does the hormone normally lower blood glucose?\n(iii) Name the hormone with the opposite effect and the treatment for Sanjay.", a: "(i) Diabetes mellitus; the hormone is insulin.\n(ii) The β-cells of the islets of Langerhans secrete it. Insulin increases the uptake of glucose by cells (hepatocytes and adipocytes) and its conversion into glycogen, so the blood glucose falls.\n(iii) Glucagon (from the α-cells), which stimulates glycogenolysis and raises blood glucose, has the opposite effect. Sanjay is treated by insulin therapy.", marks: 4, type: "case" }
    ]
  }
});
